The base nib connector is instantiated, so it cannot be abstract.

-[NSNibConnector establishConnection] was NSInvalidAbstractInvocation(), on the assumption that
only subclasses are ever created. Modern nibs create the base class directly.

Interface Builder emits a plain NSNibConnector with a NIL DESTINATION as a decode ORDERING MARKER,
one per NSStackView, and its label says so in words:

    Encoding NSStackView requires being decoded before other connections with an early decoding
    order priority of 999990.

Establishing one is meant to do nothing. Apple documents the default implementation of
establishConnection as doing nothing, and every application that puts a stack view in a nib would
die here otherwise, so the evidence for that is every shipping application.

Counted in iA Writer alone: GeneralPreferences.nib has 6, EditorPreferences.nib has 9,
CustomPatternsPreferences.nib has 0.

MEASURED, same drive before and after: 9 ABSTRACT lines to 0, with the Editor pane and the Custom
Patterns sheet rendering identically and 0 unrecognised selectors either way. It was only log noise,
because NSInvalidAbstractInvocation logs and returns rather than raising, but it is 15 lines per
full preferences visit and it says a defect where there is none.

--- a/AppKit/nib.subproj/NSNibConnector.m
+++ b/AppKit/nib.subproj/NSNibConnector.m
@@ -99,8 +99,12 @@
         [self setDestination: replacement];
 }
 
+/* THE BASE CLASS IS INSTANTIATED DIRECTLY by modern nibs, so this cannot be abstract. Interface
+ * Builder emits a plain NSNibConnector with a NIL DESTINATION as a decode ORDERING MARKER, one per
+ * NSStackView, whose label reads that encoding NSStackView requires being decoded before other
+ * connections. Establishing one is meant to do nothing, which is what Apple documents for the
+ * default implementation, and every application using a stack view would die here otherwise. */
 - (void) establishConnection {
-    NSInvalidAbstractInvocation();
 }
 
 @end
